  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Chances for rain Sunday into Monday morning, \nthen a chance of rain and snow Wednesday through Thanksgiving. \n\nThe upper level pattern becomes more active and meridional as we \nhead into the weekend and next week. Saturday into Sunday, the \narea of cloudiness currently over FL and off its east coast will\nbegin to organize into a coastal low. This system translates \nnorth along or just off the East Coast through Monday as it \ninteracts with upper level energy. Late Saturday night into \nSunday, the system makes its closest approach to the southern \nand central Appalachians, and could spread light rain into the \neastern half of the forecast area. \n\nModels including AI models are wavering on the position of the \nsurface low and whether a second wave develops to our west, as \nwell as the amount of time the system stays off coastal VA/ \nCarolina. This will no doubt mean changes to the QPF footprint \nover the next couple of days. Some models and members are \nshowing higher rainfall totals for eastern VA and NC, with only \nlight rain for Southside VA and the NC Piedmont. Still others \nspread additional rain to the entire forecast area. Will keep \nthat as a possibility, but focus most of the rain on the eastern\nhalf of the area. \n\nAhead of the system, a wedge of cold air will develop against\nthe Blue Ridge, with northerly winds across much of the area. \nThe atmosphere will have to undergo top-down saturation to \novercome dry air in place at first before precipitation can \nstart. \n\nWraparound light rain showers and stratus look to persist into \nMonday in the western mountains. Colder weather returns for the \nrest of the week, with a cold wedge re-establishing itself along\nthe Blue Ridge around Thanksgiving. At the same time, possible \nphasing systems could support another round of surface \ncyclogenesis and widespread QPF along the East Coast. The \nThanksgiving upper low looks to dig deep into the SE U.S., which\nwould put us in an area of stronger mid and upper level \nforcing. Thus the possibility for a chilly, somewhat windy, and \nwet Thanksgiving is increasing. With this system, we would have \nenough cold air for a mix of rain and snow in parts of the area.",
